**Approach:** Use the Composite Graph API (`/services/data/vXX.X/composite/graph`, available since v53). It's the right fit here because:

- Each graph is a **unit of work** — the account upsert and the opportunity insert for one order either both commit or both roll back.
- Graphs are processed **independently**: a failure in one graph never prevents the other graphs in the same call from being saved (unlike a plain Composite call, where subrequests can't be grouped atomically without `allOrNone=true`, which would roll back everything).
- The middleware can match each order back by the `referenceId`s, and within a graph the opportunity links to its account via the `@{acct_XXXX.id}` reference to the upsert result.

Each graph upserts the account on the external ID field with `PATCH /sobjects/Account/Customer_Number__c/<value>` (creates it if it doesn't exist), then creates the opportunity in the same graph, referencing the account's record ID from the upsert result.

```http
POST /services/data/v60.0/composite/graph HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "graphs": [
    {
      "graphId": "order_SO-1001",
      "compositeRequest": [
        {
          "method": "PATCH",
          "url": "/services/data/v60.0/sobjects/Account/Customer_Number__c/C-1001",
          "referenceId": "acct_1001",
          "body": {
            "Name": "Alder Creek Cafe",
            "Customer_Number__c": "C-1001"
          }
        },
        {
          "method": "POST",
          "url": "/services/data/v60.0/sobjects/Opportunity",
          "referenceId": "opp_1001",
          "body": {
            "Name": "Web order SO-1001",
            "StageName": "Closed Won",
            "CloseDate": "2026-09-26",
            "Amount": 1250,
            "AccountId": "@{acct_1001.id}"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "graphId": "order_SO-1002",
      "compositeRequest": [
        {
          "method": "PATCH",
          "url": "/services/data/v60.0/sobjects/Account/Customer_Number__c/C-2002",
          "referenceId": "acct_1002",
          "body": {
            "Name": "Birch Lane Bakery",
            "Customer_Number__c": "C-2002"
          }
        },
        {
          "method": "POST",
          "url": "/services/data/v60.0/sobjects/Opportunity",
          "referenceId": "opp_1002",
          "body": {
            "Name": "Web order SO-1002",
            "StageName": "Closed Won",
            "CloseDate": "2026-09-26",
            "Amount": 980,
            "AccountId": "@{acct_1002.id}"
          }
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

A single Composite REST API call handles this: `allOrNone: true` makes it transactional (any failure rolls back everything), and composite subrequests execute sequentially in order, so the Asset is committed before the `Service_Request__c` insert fires its trigger — the trigger's SerialNumber query will find the Asset. The Account relationship on the Asset uses the referenceId merge field `@{newAccount.id}`.

```http
POST /services/data/v50.0/composite HTTP/1.1
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "allOrNone": true,
  "compositeRequest": [
    {
      "method": "POST",
      "url": "/services/data/v50.0/sobjects/Account",
      "referenceId": "newAccount",
      "body": {
        "Name": "Hollis Outdoor Supply"
      }
    },
    {
      "method": "POST",
      "url": "/services/data/v50.0/sobjects/Asset",
      "referenceId": "newAsset",
      "body": {
        "Name": "Trailhead Kiosk 3000",
        "SerialNumber": "SN-88213",
        "AccountId": "@{newAccount.id}"
      }
    },
    {
      "method": "POST",
      "url": "/services/data/v50.0/sobjects/Service_Request__c",
      "referenceId": "newRequest",
      "body": {
        "Subject__c": "Installation",
        "Serial_Number__c": "SN-88213"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```
